{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-categorical-connection","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Definition","doco:Section"],"label":"def:categorical-connection","kind":"definition","title":"Categorical connection and curvature","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-higher-forms"],"examples":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/ex-connection-su2","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/ex-categorical-connection-su2"],"tags":["connection","curvature"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.CategoricalConnection","text":"categorical-connection.md","leanSource":"categorical-connection.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"def:categorical-connection\"></a><p>Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(\\mathbf{C}, A, \\tau)</code> be a quantum universe over <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">R</code> with differential forms <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Omega^k(A)</code> (<a href=\"#def:higher-forms\" data-at=\"quantum-universes/quantum-universe-frobenius-pairing-unique\">higher forms</a>).</p>\n<p>A <strong>categorical connection</strong> on <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A</code> is a morphism <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nabla \\colon A \\to A \\otimes \\Omega^1(A)</code> in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathbf{C}</code> satisfying the Leibniz rule <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nabla(\\mu(a \\otimes b)) = \\nabla(a) \\cdot b + a \\cdot \\nabla(b)</code>. It generalizes the classical de Rham connection <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">d</code> (the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{SU}(2)</code> instance in <a href=\"#ex:categorical-connection-su2\" data-at=\"quantum-universes/qu-structures\">Example</a> gives a concrete picture). The rule is expressed by the commutativity of the <em>Leibniz triangle</em>:</p>\n<div class=\"tex-ph\"><strong>[Diagram]</strong><details><summary>LaTeX source</summary><pre>\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:leibniz-triangle}\n\\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=2.5em, column sep=3.5em]\n  A \\otimes A\n    \\ar[r, &quot;\\mu&quot;]\n    \\ar[dr, &quot;{\\nabla \\otimes 1 + 1 \\otimes \\nabla}&quot;']\n  &amp; A \\ar[d, &quot;\\nabla&quot;]\n  \\\\\n  &amp; A \\otimes \\Omega^1(A)\n\\end{tikzcd}\n\\end{equation}</pre></details></div>\n<p>The <strong>curvature</strong> of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nabla</code> is the composition:</p>\n<figure class=\"tex-svg\"><img data-src=\"rendered/quantum-universes/categorical-connection-1.svg\" alt=\"\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:curvature-composition}\n\\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=3em]\n  A \\ar[r, &quot;\\nabla&quot;]\n  &amp; A \\otimes \\Omega^1(A) \\ar[r, &quot;\\nabla&quot;]\n  &amp; A \\otimes \\Omega^2(A)\n\\end{tikzcd}\n\\end{equation}\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p>This composition is denoted <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">F_\\nabla = \\nabla^2</code>. It is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A</code>-linear, a standard consequence of the Leibniz rule.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/categorical-connection.md","heading":"Definition","number":"3.6.1","page":"b/def-categorical-connection","qa":{"counts":{"pass":88,"fail":0,"warn":1,"na":13,"unknown":0,"stale":77},"milnor":{"total":3,"notPassing":1}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"qou:QOU.CategoricalConnection","via":"sibling","path":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/categorical-connection.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
